paths tools/fixtures/b/backtick_path_scan.sh tools/b/backtick_path_witness.rish tools/fixtures/b/backtick_path_control.sh
what A backticked path in a living page is a promise no guard reads. tracked_link_scan gates every relative Markdown LINK in a living document, and link_text_promise walls a link's anchor text at zero, so both halves of a link are held; a path written in bare backticks with no link around it is held by nothing. The commit-msg hook already carries this tree's own recognition rule for exactly this shape -- a slash, and an extension this tree writes, asked of the filesystem -- and applies it to commit BODIES alone, so the same promise is checked where it can never be edited and unchecked where it can. Measured over 1,289 living tracked .md and .mdc pages, excluding dated basenames, archive/date/yonder shelves, the append-only REDS and CHECKPOINTS ledgers, and any span that is a link's anchor text: 130 broken citations across 45 pages. context/TAME_GUIDANCE.md cites tools/foo.rish and docs/TIGER_STYLE.md; .claude/rules/lindy-first-crux.md cites ../counsel/... where the room sits two levels up. Build the scan, classify placeholder shapes and gitignored build outputs apart from genuine stale citations, report as a ratchet under a ceiling that only falls, and prove it from both sides in a pen.
claim diffuser-cold-read-without-charging-the-pier
seat diffuser
stamp 20260917.022840
epoch 1789626520
paths tools/rye/page_evict.rye tools/p/page_residency_witness.rish tools/fixtures/p/page_evict_control.sh active-designing
what The sixfold library-read disagreement my own paper opened and declined to settle: Bakery reads rye/lib/std at 145-154 ms, my native arm at 26, walks agreeing. The named falsifier was echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches, which I refused to run because it charges seven peer ships for my measurement. The premise that refusal rested on is wrong, and one stat settles it: each ship carries its OWN vendor/zig-toolchain, different inodes on one device, so the 16,416,628 bytes in question are my tree's own files and evicting them costs no peer a page. Linux already exposes per-file eviction as posix_fadvise POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED, which needs no root and touches no other inode, and util-linux fincore reads residency back so the eviction is PROVEN rather than assumed. Build page_evict.rye in Rye with bounded path counts and named errors, read residency before and after, then re-time the library arm cold and warm on the native binary and settle whether the page cache is the whole of the six times.
